HI_T15 = 'Question 1 — Examine — 15 marks\nWhat the question wants: a close examination of the reasons for the Soviet intervention, with each reason tested against evidence and the reasons weighed against one another, not one reason asserted.\nWhat earned credit: The reform sequence is dated in order, and the paragraph gives a specific mechanism: "Moscow\'s fear that Dubček\'s reforms would spread". The April 1968 Action Programme and the June "Two Thousand Words" manifesto are correctly placed.\nProvisional mark: 5–6 of 15 (band 4–6), provisional, for this paragraph read as the body of a whole essay.\nBiggest gap: the paragraph\'s own evidence describes fear of contagion and bloc cohesion, yet it concludes "mainly because of ideology" and weighs no other reason, so the demand to examine the reasons is not met.\nFix it: 1) Correct the facts first. Dubček became First Secretary in January 1968, not 1967. The invasion began on the night of 20–21 August 1968, not September. Romania did not take part, so "every Warsaw Pact country" is wrong. The Brezhnev Doctrine was formulated after the invasion, in September 1968; the July warnings came through the Warsaw letter and the Bratislava meeting. The claim that the reforms let other parties form goes beyond what the Action Programme achieved before August. Remove the claim about protests in East Germany and the troop figure unless your textbook gives them. 2) Add at least two more reasons, each as a chain from evidence to outcome, for example the threat to Warsaw Pact cohesion and Czechoslovakia\'s strategic place in the bloc. Test each against evidence that points the other way, such as the negotiations at Čierna nad Tisou in July 1968. 3) Replace "Therefore the Soviet Union invaded Czechoslovakia mainly because of ideology" with a weighed judgement at a stated extent: which reason explains the decision to invade, and which explains its timing.\nModel: Ideology supplied the justification, but the reforms\' threat to the bloc\'s cohesion is the reason Moscow acted in August rather than tolerating them. (A structure to test against your textbook, not a sentence to submit.)\nAssumption: previous syllabus, because your exams are in May 2027, and the paragraph is marked as the body of a 15-mark essay. This session has no textbook library, so the dates above are course-standard knowledge; check them against your textbook before you rely on them.\nNext step: rewrite the paragraph with the corrected dates and at least two reasons, and send it back for marking.'
HI_T15_DOC = ('doc-prague-student', 'My paragraph (student work, supplied text)', 'Student', "One reason for the Soviet intervention in Czechoslovakia in 1968 was Moscow's fear that Dubček's reforms would spread to the rest of the Eastern bloc. Alexander Dubček became First Secretary of the Czechoslovak Communist Party in January 1967 and introduced 'socialism with a human face', which ended censorship and allowed other political parties to form. In April 1968 the party published its Action Programme, and in June the 'Two Thousand Words' manifesto called for even faster change. Brezhnev was worried because Poland and East Germany were already facing protests, so he announced the Brezhnev Doctrine in July 1968 to warn Dubček that the USSR would intervene if socialism was threatened. When Dubček refused to back down, around 250,000 troops from every Warsaw Pact country invaded on the night of 20–21 September 1968. This shows that the main reason for the invasion was ideological, because the Soviet Union could not accept a communist country choosing its own path. Therefore the Soviet Union invaded Czechoslovakia mainly because of ideology.")
ESS_P9C_Q = "I'm an ESS HL student (the course first assessed in 2026; my exams are in May 2027). Paper 2 Section A practice. You have received the Geography specialist's analysis of two storms on the Wendle Brook through the hub; it is supplied to you as the document peer:ge-p9c. Use the lag times and the percentage change in peak discharge exactly as the Geography specialist gives them, quoting each value you use and attributing it to the Geography specialist. The Wendle Brook's catchment above the gauging station has an area of 24 km². For each storm, the volume of storm runoff that passed the gauging station (the discharge above base flow) was measured: Storm 1 (38 mm of rain), 1.82 × 10⁵ m³; Storm 2 (36 mm of rain), 4.10 × 10⁵ m³. (a) Calculate the volume of rain that fell on the catchment in each storm, in m³. [2] (b) Calculate the percentage of the rainfall that left the catchment as storm runoff in each storm. [2] (c) Using the Geography specialist's results and your answers to (b), explain how the growth of the built-up area has changed the flows and storages of the catchment's water system. [4] (d) Evaluate one strategy for reducing flood risk in the catchment from the perspectives of two different environmental value systems. [4] Show your working with units."
ESS_P9C = 'Assumption: HL, ungraded Paper 2 Section A practice, so a model answer is given. The [1] tags show where the marks lie; they are not the official markscheme.\n\n(a) [2]\nRainfall depth: 38 mm = 0.038 m (Storm 1); 36 mm = 0.036 m (Storm 2)\nCatchment area: 24 km² = 24 × 10⁶ m² (1 km² = 1000 m × 1000 m = 10⁶ m²) = 2.4 × 10⁷ m²\nVolume of rain = depth × area\nStorm 1: 0.038 m × 2.4 × 10⁷ m² = 9.12 × 10⁵ m³ [1]\nStorm 2: 0.036 m × 2.4 × 10⁷ m² = 8.64 × 10⁵ m³ [1]\n\n(b) [2]\nPercentage of rain that left as storm runoff = runoff volume ÷ rain volume × 100\nStorm 1: 1.82 × 10⁵ m³ ÷ 9.12 × 10⁵ m³ × 100 = 19.956%, so 20.0% (3 s.f.) [1]\nStorm 2: 4.10 × 10⁵ m³ ÷ 8.64 × 10⁵ m³ × 100 = 47.4537%, so 47.5% (3 s.f.) [1]\nThe share rose from 20.0% to 47.5%, a rise of 27.5 percentage points.\n\n(c) [4]\nGrowth of the built-up area reduced the catchment\'s storage of water and sent more of it quickly into the brook, so the flow at the gauging station rose and arrived sooner.\n\nLess rain is stored in the ground. Roofs, roads and car parks replace permeable ground, so less rain infiltrates and is held in the soil store [1].\n\nMore rain moves as surface flow. The share of rain leaving as storm runoff rose from 20.0% in Storm 1 to 47.5% in Storm 2 (part (b)), so a larger flow reached the channel over the surface [1].\n\nWater arrives sooner. The Geography specialist reports a "lag time of 12 hours for Storm 1 and 4.5 hours for Storm 2". Drains and sewers move water off roofs and roads into the brook more quickly than soil would, and the Geography specialist notes that "Both storms had peak rainfall at 09:30, so the difference in timing comes from the catchment itself." [1]\n\nPeak flow rises. The Geography specialist reports that "the peak discharge rose by 200%, from 5.4 m³ s⁻¹ in Storm 1 to 16.2 m³ s⁻¹ in Storm 2". The larger, faster surface flow reaches the channel together, so the peak rises [1].\n\nThe Geography specialist also notes that "the data do not record soil moisture before each storm, which could also have differed". The mechanism is established, but two storms show a consistent pattern rather than proof that the built-up area caused the change.\n\n(d) [4]\nStrategy: sustainable drainage, such as permeable paving, green roofs and rain gardens, which holds rain on the surface and lets it soak into the ground, restoring some of the storage that built surfaces removed [1].\n\nEcocentric view: an ecocentric view values the brook\'s natural processes and habitats, so it would favour features that work with infiltration and vegetation rather than hard channels, with low impact on the stream [1].\n\nTechnocentric view: a technocentric view trusts engineering it can design and measure, so it would question whether small, spread-out features can hold back the largest storms, and would probably prefer engineered storage of known capacity [1].\n\nJudgement: sustainable drainage is a partial solution. It can reduce surface runoff and soften peaks in moderate storms, but on its own it cannot undo a built-up share that rose from 8% to 31%, and these data give no figure for how much it would reduce the peak, so it is most convincing as one part of a combined plan [1].\n\nNext step: write each perspective as one claim with a figure from part (b), then test it against the limit you named.\n\nSources: your question and data (supplied text); the Geography specialist\'s report on the Wendle Brook storms, quoted exactly (supplied text); course notes on urban drainage, sustainable drainage and value systems (course notes)\n'
HI_P8C_Q = "A literature student is reading a poem set in Montgomery, Alabama, during the bus boycott. For that student, write one paragraph of no more than 200 words outlining the historical context: the arrest of Rosa Parks and the start of the boycott in December 1955, how the boycott was organised and sustained, and how and when it ended in December 1956, with dated and quantified evidence. (I'm an SL History student sitting my exams in May 2027, on the syllabus first assessed in 2017, and I study Paper 1 prescribed subject 4, Rights and protest; this is a non-assessed short answer.)"
HI_P8C = "Rosa Parks, a member of the Montgomery branch of the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP), was arrested on 1 December 1955 for refusing to give up her bus seat to a white passenger. The Women's Political Council, led by Jo Ann Robinson, circulated leaflets calling for a boycott, which began on 5 December 1955, the day of Parks's trial. That evening, Black leaders formed the Montgomery Improvement Association (MIA) and chose the Baptist minister Martin Luther King Jr. as its president. Carpool networks, volunteer drivers and mass meetings sustained the boycott for 381 days, until 20 December 1956. The end came after the Supreme Court had upheld, in November 1956, a federal ruling against segregated buses, and the order reached Montgomery. The boycott suggests that everyday, collective refusal, organised locally, could build the pressure that led to legal change.\n\nSources: none consulted (no retrieval in this session)"
ESS_P9C_PEER_DOC = ('peer:ge-p9c', 'Geography specialist (relayed report)', 'geography-specialist', 'Paper 1, Option A (Freshwater), structured question. The Wendle Brook, Marsh Lane Bridge and both storms are fictional practice data.\n\n(a) [2]\nLag time = time of peak discharge − time of peak rainfall\nStorm 1: 21:30 − 09:30 = 12 hours\nStorm 2: 14:00 − 09:30 = 4 hours 30 minutes = 4.5 hours\nResult: lag time of 12 hours for Storm 1 and 4.5 hours for Storm 2.\n\n(b) [2]\nPercentage change = (new − old) ÷ old × 100\n= (16.2 − 5.4) ÷ 5.4 × 100\n= 10.8 ÷ 5.4 × 100\n= 200%\nResult: the peak discharge rose by 200%, from 5.4 m³ s⁻¹ in Storm 1 to 16.2 m³ s⁻¹ in Storm 2. This is a threefold increase (16.2 ÷ 5.4 = 3.0).\n\n(c) [2]\nDischarge Q = cross-sectional area × mean velocity\nCross-sectional area = width × mean depth = 6.0 m × 1.8 m = 10.8 m²\nQ = 10.8 m² × 1.5 m s⁻¹ = 16.2 m³ s⁻¹\nResult: the calculated discharge is 16.2 m³ s⁻¹. This agrees with the recorded peak discharge of 16.2 m³ s⁻¹ at 14:00 in Storm 2.\n\n(d) [4]\nFirst way: more surface runoff gives a higher peak. Roofs, roads and car parks replace permeable ground, so less rain soaks into the soil and more runs straight off the surface. A larger volume of water reaches the channel in a short time, so the peak discharge rises. Between 2006 and 2024 the built-up share of the catchment rose from 8% to 31%, and Storm 2 produced a peak 200% higher than Storm 1 from similar rainfall (36 mm against 38 mm). The larger peak is therefore consistent with the change in the catchment, although the data do not record soil moisture before each storm, which could also have differed.\n\nSecond way: drains and sewers shorten the lag time. Gutters, storm drains and sewers carry water from roofs and roads to the brook faster than the ground would, so water reaches the channel sooner after the rain. Both storms had peak rainfall at 09:30, so the difference in timing comes from the catchment itself. The lag time fell from 12 hours in 2006 to 4.5 hours in 2024, which is consistent with a more developed drainage network.\n\nNext step: sketch both hydrographs on one set of axes, with time in hours, discharge in m³ s⁻¹ and rainfall in mm, and label the two lag times.\n\nSources: Wendle Brook storm data, catchment area and built-up shares (supplied text); general Geography course knowledge (course notes)')

