LA_3 = '\nTask — Paper 2, SL — how and why two works present a character excluded from their community\n\nWhat the task wants: a comparison of how and why two works present exclusion, argued inside every paragraph.\n\nWhat earned credit: accurate plot knowledge of both works, and a relevant quotation, "misery made me a fiend", linked to the creature\'s exclusion.\n\nProvisional marks: A 3–4/10 · B1 2/5 · B2 1–2/5 · C 2–3/5 · D 3/5 — total 11–14/30 — provisional\n\nWhy:\n- A 3–4: both plots are known, but "why" is never answered; the interpretation stops at "exclusion is bad".\n- B1 2: "Shelley uses lots of imagery and emotive language here to show he is lonely" names features and asserts an effect; no word is analysed.\n- B2 1–2: the works are told one after the other; "both books about people who are excluded" is asserted, not analysed.\n- C 2–3: a clear introduction, two work-based paragraphs and a conclusion, but the question\'s "how" and "why" do not shape the structure.\n- D 3: clear and mostly accurate; register slips ("lots of", "very sad", "very effective"); spell the author\'s name Brontë.\n\nBiggest gap: no paragraph compares the works. Each reports plot, then names a device, so the essay describes rather than answers "how and why".\n\nFix it:\n1. Plan three body paragraphs, each opening with one comparative point about both works, such as how each outsider is refused.\n2. In each paragraph, choose one exact moment per work and explain one key word: what "misery" does to the creature\'s account of himself, then the matching moment in Jane\'s narration.\n3. Replace "Both books are very effective." and "Shelley and Bronte both want to show that exclusion is bad" with claims a moment supports; the second guesses intention.\n\nModel: "Both novels exclude their outsider through appearance or status, but Shelley\'s creature is refused for how he looks, while Brontë\'s Jane is refused for her dependence, so the two works locate the cause of exclusion in different places."\n\nConfidence: lower; one essay, no texts or official criteria supplied, quotations unchecked.\n'
